---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Btn from '../components/Btn.astro';
const updated = '8 August 2026';
---
<Base title="How we test" description="The method behind the fee figures published here, and the rubric software reviews will be scored against when they begin.">
<div class="wrap"><Breadcrumbs trail={[{ label: 'How we test', href: '/how-we-test/' }]} /></div>
<section class="wrap" style="padding:1.5rem 1.25rem 0">
  <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
  <h1 style="max-width:20ch">How we check a figure</h1>
  <p style="max-width:46ch;font-size:1.12rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem">
    Most of the work behind a page on this site is not writing. It is establishing which of four
    published values for the same fee is the current one, and who has an interest in the answer.
  </p>
  <p class="note" style="margin:0 0 2rem">Last updated {updated}.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The four checks every figure goes through</h2>

    <h3>1. Where did the number originate</h3>
    <p>
      Trace it back until you reach a platform announcement, a help page, a regulation or a government
      publication. A figure in an article that cites another article is not sourced, it is repeated. Most
      marketplace fee content is at least two hops from a primary source and never says so.
    </p>

    <h3>2. What date does it belong to</h3>
    <p>
      Fee content ages badly and is rarely revisited. During research for the reimbursement guide on this
      site, three different effective dates for the same policy change were found in circulation, one of
      which corresponds to nothing the platform published. A rate is a statement about a moment; the
      moment gets recorded alongside it.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Who benefits from the number being what it is</h3>
    <p>
      This is the check that changes conclusions most often. If every published estimate of a loss comes
      from companies that sell recovery software, that is not a consensus, it is a marketing category. The
      publisher of each secondary source is named in our source lists so the reader can apply the same
      discount we did.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Do the sources agree</h3>
    <p>
      When they do not — and on this subject they frequently do not — the disagreement is published rather
      than resolved. Several pages here carry a table of contested figures for exactly this reason.
      Presenting one value as settled when four are in circulation is how the problem propagates.
    </p>

    <h2>What that produces on a page</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What it guarantees</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sources block with retrieval dates</td><td>Every claim can be checked at its origin, and you can see how old the check is</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Publisher named for secondary sources</td><td>You can see who had a commercial interest in the figure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Contested-figures table</td><td>Where sources conflict, you get both values instead of one confident guess</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Illustrative figures labelled</td><td>You can tell a demonstration of arithmetic from a quoted rate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Published and modified dates</td><td>You can judge whether a page has been revisited since the last fee change</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Software reviews</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>No software reviews are published on this site yet.</strong> The rubric below is published in
      advance so that the standard exists before the first review does, rather than being written to fit a
      conclusion afterwards.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Weight</th><th>What earns a high score</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Data accuracy</td><td>30%</td><td>Figures reconcile against the platform's own reports on a sampled basis, with the sample stated</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Operational fit</td><td>25%</td><td>Solves a problem a working seller actually has, without a workaround</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost at real volume</td><td>20%</td><td>Total cost stays predictable as order volume grows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Setup and support</td><td>15%</td><td>Time to first useful output; support answers operational questions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Data portability</td><td>10%</td><td>Exports are complete and usable outside the tool</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Conditions that will apply</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Tools are tested on paid plans bought at list price. Vendor-supplied review accounts are declined.</li>
      <li>Every review states the plan tier tested and the month of testing.</li>
      <li>No score is assigned to a tool that has not been run on real data.</li>
      <li>Scores are revisited when a vendor changes pricing or ships a material change.</li>
      <li>No sponsored reviews, no paid placement, no vendor sight of a review before publication.</li>
      <li>If an affiliate relationship exists with a reviewed tool, it is disclosed on the review and is never a scoring input.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Errors are corrected in place with a dated note and logged on the{' '}
      <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>. Nothing is edited silently.
    </p>

    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <Btn href="/editorial-standards/">Editorial standards</Btn>
      <Btn href="/policy-log/" quiet={true}>See the dated change log</Btn>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
